    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 7\n    nc = 8\n    facility_coords = [(87, 66), (16, 71), (77, 65), (61, 2), (83, 38), (80, 52), (85, 15)]\n    customer_coords = [(71, 37), (55, 94), (70, 7), (5, 19), (55, 47), (94, 76), (66, 64), (3, 92)]\n    capacities = [57.0, 77.0, 107.0, 124.0, 152.0, 168.0, 87.0]\n    demands = [38.0, 15.0, 11.0, 15.0, 39.0, 50.0, 46.0, 24.0]\n    fixed_costs = [422.0, 178.0, 298.0, 378.0, 328.0, 499.0, 441.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: F0 requires F6\n    m.addConstr(open_f[0] <= open_f[6])\n\n    # Policy: opening budget cap $1411\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 1411.0)\n\n    # Policy: min throughput 10 per open facility\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) >= 10.0*open_f[i])\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Steering Committee\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse Activation and Customer Fulfillment Decision \u2014 Business Requirements and Planning Data\n\nPurpose and Decision Context\nThe company is determining which candidate warehouses to activate and how to route outbound shipments so that all customers are served reliably at the lowest total cost. Candidate facility locations and customer locations are specified as map coordinates in the annex. The decision covers both site activation and shipment allocations.\n\nCost and Service Principles\n- Customer service: Every customer\u2019s demand for the planning period must be fully satisfied; no shortfalls are permitted.\n- Activation and usage: Shipments may originate only from facilities the company chooses to activate. Closed sites will not handle any outbound volume.\n- Capacity discipline: Each facility has a defined throughput limit. Total outbound volume assigned to any activated facility must remain within its available capacity.\n- Cost structure: Activating a facility incurs a fixed cost. Transportation spend depends on the distance between the selected facility and the customer and on the units shipped. A per\u2011kilometer, per\u2011unit transport rate is provided in the annex. Distances will be based on the mapped locations provided, and the same rate applies uniformly across all lanes.\n- Objective: Select the set of facilities to activate and the associated shipment plan that meet all service and capacity requirements while minimizing the sum of fixed activation costs and variable transportation costs.\n\nMandatory Company Policies\n- Regional dependency: The candidate site labeled F0 at (87, 66) relies on the regional hub at F6 at (85, 15) for shared infrastructure. If F0 is activated, F6 must also be activated.\n- Capital discipline for activation: The capital budget for facility activation is capped at $1411. The combined fixed costs of all facilities selected for activation must not exceed this amount.\n- Minimum utilization threshold: To justify operating overhead, every activated facility must handle at least 10 units of total outbound shipments within the planning period. Any site projected to ship less than this threshold should remain closed.\n\nPlanning Scope and Implementation Notes\n- The decision framework allows customers to be supplied from one or multiple activated facilities if that improves total cost while honoring capacity and policy requirements.\n- The variable transport rate in the annex will be applied consistently to the distance and volume moved on each facility\u2013customer lane.\n- All fulfillment and siting decisions should be made for the planning horizon covered by the demand, capacity, and cost inputs listed below.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended facilities to activate, demonstrating adherence to the capital budget and dependency and utilization policies.\n- Customer-to-facility shipment plan that fully satisfies demand, stays within capacity, and minimizes total landed cost.\n- Summary of expected total spend segmented into activation costs and transportation costs.\n\nAnnex: Planning Data (placeholders)\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (87, 66)\n  - F1: (16, 71)\n  - F2: (77, 65)\n  - F3: (61, 2)\n  - F4: (83, 38)\n  - F5: (80, 52)\n  - F6: (85, 15)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (71, 37)\n  - C1: (55, 94)\n  - C2: (70, 7)\n  - C3: (5, 19)\n  - C4: (55, 47)\n  - C5: (94, 76)\n  - C6: (66, 64)\n  - C7: (3, 92)\n- Facility capacities (units for the planning period): [57.0, 77.0, 107.0, 124.0, 152.0, 168.0, 87.0]\n- Customer demands (units for the planning period): [38.0, 15.0, 11.0, 15.0, 39.0, 50.0, 46.0, 24.0]\n- Facility activation fixed costs (currency units): [422.0, 178.0, 298.0, 378.0, 328.0, 499.0, 441.0]\n- Transport rate (currency per km per unit): 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Steering Committee\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Warehouse Activation and Customer Fulfillment Decision \u2014 Business Requirements and Planning Data\n\nPurpose and Decision Context\nThe company is determining which candidate warehouses to activate and how to route outbound shipments so that all customers are served reliably at the lowest total cost. Candidate facility locations and customer locations are specified as map coordinates in the annex. The decision covers both site activation and shipment allocations.\n\nCost and Service Principles\n- Customer service: Every customer\u2019s demand for the planning period must be fully satisfied; no shortfalls are permitted.\n- Activation and usage: Shipments may originate only from facilities the company chooses to activate. Closed sites will not handle any outbound volume.\n- Capacity discipline: Each facility has a defined throughput limit. Total outbound volume assigned to any activated facility must remain within its available capacity.\n- Cost structure: Activating a facility incurs a fixed cost. Transportation spend depends on the distance between the selected facility and the customer and on the units shipped. A per\u2011kilometer, per\u2011unit transport rate is provided in the annex. Distances will be based on the mapped locations provided, and the same rate applies uniformly across all lanes.\n- Objective: Select the set of facilities to activate and the associated shipment plan that meet all service and capacity requirements while minimizing the sum of fixed activation costs and variable transportation costs.\n\nMandatory Company Policies\n- Regional dependency: The candidate site labeled F0 at (87, 66) relies on the regional hub at F6 at (85, 15) for shared infrastructure. If F0 is activated, F6 must also be activated.\n- Capital discipline for activation: The capital budget for facility activation is capped at $1411. The combined fixed costs of all facilities selected for activation must not exceed this amount.\n- Minimum utilization threshold: To justify operating overhead, every activated facility must handle at least 10 units of total outbound shipments within the planning period. Any site projected to ship less than this threshold should remain closed.\n\nPlanning Scope and Implementation Notes\n- The decision framework allows customers to be supplied from one or multiple activated facilities if that improves total cost while honoring capacity and policy requirements.\n- The variable transport rate in the annex will be applied consistently to the distance and volume moved on each facility\u2013customer lane.\n- All fulfillment and siting decisions should be made for the planning horizon covered by the demand, capacity, and cost inputs listed below.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended facilities to activate, demonstrating adherence to the capital budget and dependency and utilization policies.\n- Customer-to-facility shipment plan that fully satisfies demand, stays within capacity, and minimizes total landed cost.\n- Summary of expected total spend segmented into activation costs and transportation costs.\n\nAnnex: Planning Data (placeholders)\n- Candidate facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities (units for the planning period): {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demands (units for the planning period): {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Facility activation fixed costs (currency units): {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate (currency per km per unit): {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
